A||N||7||Seanh||Worth it to rebuild brake calipers yourself?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||04-01-2001||01:56 AM||||Just wondering. My rear brakes are shot, dont even grip at all, and while I am working, I figured I would rebuilt the calipers while im at it. First off, I am pretty sure they work, but seeing as I have no other drivable fiero, I have nothing to compare it to. Should I even rebuild them at all? I am going to do a GrandAm vented upgrade on the front, but not back becuase I want to keep the stock ebrake system. If you think I should rebuild them, should I use the Fiero store rebuild kit? Is it a good price? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||04-01-2001||03:04 AM||||Easy job...do it. I did it on my Formula and on my 86 GT. I (obivously) replaced the pads, honed out the piston cylinder, and bought a rebuild kit from Autozone, which basically consist of o-rings and such. They work like new now. M7F<P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero Se V6 4 spd<BR>1986 Fiero GT 5 spd<BR>1988 Fiero Formula auto<BR>2001 Mustang 5 spd||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fierowrecker||04-01-2001||08:08 AM||||Hey Sean!<BR>If your stock e-brake system still works, you are a lucky son of a gun!<BR>I did the Grand Am upgrade on the back using NAPA parts, only cost $144 for the pair of rotors and loaded calipers...<BR>Course, here in Michigan, we never have e-brakes after the car sees it's first winter...<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||04-01-2001||09:27 AM||||Yes, my stock ebrake system still works, and I haven't done a thing to it. Except the fact my pads are thinner then a sheet of paper, it works perfectly. Do you think I should convert it to vented rotors? I never find myself using the ebrake, but I just like knowing its there. We don't have inspections here, so that isn't a big problem. Maybe I'll do the back in vented and keep all the stock Fiero parts incase I want it back. <P>What parts off which year car do I need for the rear vented conversion? Is it a direct bolt on to the old system? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-01-2001||02:01 PM||||read the brake upgrade article in my cave... (In body & Chassis.)<P>If the calipers are working properly and all that then leave the OE rear system intact.<P>If the E brake is working well then caliper rebuild is an option. Take your time with them and it can be done with good results. The picky part is the e brake screw and the check valve in the piston face. (Not all pistons have the valve...) Usually the e brake screw hole is messed up beyond the ability of most people to fix it.<P>You don't need to rebuild them just because the pads are shot. They only need that kind of work if there are seal or internal problems with the thing.<P>before you even decide this see if you can get the bleed screws open. Everything else is determined after that.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||04-01-2001||03:13 PM||||I just got done reading it, WoW! That was long, but I think I understand the jist of it. I see how you bring up the point that rotor sits so close to the rock gard, that airflow is restriced and vented rotors are kinda useless when there is no air flow. Just like a radiator is nothing without any airflow thru it. What if I were to just rebuild all 4 calipers, put on all new semi-matalic pads, either buy new, or have mine resurfaced(not sure which to do yet, what you think?).<P>What are your opinions on the Fiero Store "Big Bore Master Cylinder"? It is the same price as a rebuilt stock one, so why not go with it instead? I want to replace or rebuild the majority of my brake system, if I upgrade or not. I know my eBrake works, somewhat, I dont know if it is in perfect condition or not, but I wish to rebuild the calipers now, while I have the car apart to prevent any future problems.<P>What's the point of trying to unscrew the bleeder screw? Do the threads usally strip out after a while or what? If they are, cant I take it to a machine shop and have them tap it or put a new sleeve in?||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||04-01-2001||05:45 PM||||It's long... and that is the short version. That's condensed from over a year of research and a couple thousand pages of documents.<P>Reread the thing again.... It really is easier if you print it. read over what people have said in the "Brake Upgrade Math" thread as well.<P>Last thing you want on the OE system is a bigger MC bore. It's not the way to go with the front only GA upgrade either. I will NEVER recomend the GA rear version for a street driven car.<P>Fierostore is dangerously incompetant when dealing with brake issues. They are playing Russian Roulette with their products based on the Grand Am upgrade. So is anyone else selling this group of products. F'store's big MC is the same as the Full Size Blazer MC you read about in other articles.<P>Using a larger MC will reduce brake power. This applies to both stock and GA brakes. The larger bore is used because the Fiero MC runs into trouble when all 4 wheels are using GA calipers. (This is a hydraulic volume problem. Increasing volume by increasing MC diam ALWAYS REDUCES pressure at the calipers.)<P>You want to make sure the bleeders open before you rebuild. If they won't open or break then check Autozones prices on rebuilt vs parts plus having to fight with broken bleeders.<P>The main issue with bleeders in Fiero is electrolytic corosion. Same thing you have problems with on boats with diferant kinds of metals. This isn't like normal rust. It creates some really bizar metal alloys. The result is that the bleeders are effectivly welded in place. The fix is, as you guessed, to drill them out and use bleeder repair kits. That cost money to do.<P>In other words.... the cost of bleeder repair + cost of seal kit + time often equals or exceedes cost of rebuilt parts available thru retail. Plus the retail rebuilts have a warantee on them. usually one to three years.<P><BR>The OE system, when properly maintained, will work very well. Keep in mind that solid rear rotors are the norm in this weight class. Even on many very expensive cars.<P>Solid front rotors only become an issue in certain conditions. If you choose good brake pads the solid front works much better than most people give it credit.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||04-01-2001||05:51 PM||||Awesome, I understand now.<P>I will probally get a stock, rebuilt MC from FieroStore, or Advance Auto here. I will check prices first. I see the seal kits from FieroStore are pretty cheap, but I see they dont make a total rebuild kit for the front. Only the little seal kit, which doesn't include a new piston or anything. I will check Advanced tonight and see what they charge for rebuilt calipers vs. rebuilding them myself. Thanks<P>||||reg||1
